-- 50 additional Discrete Mathematics exam questions
-- Covers: set theory, logic, proofs, combinatorics, recurrences, number theory,
--         relations, functions, graph theory, Boolean algebra, complexity
-- ── SET THEORY (5) ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('How many elements are in the power set of a set with 4 elements?', '16', '8', '4', '32', '16', 'The power set of an n-element set has 2^n elements; 2^4 = 16.', 760, 'DiscreteMath'),
('If |A| = 10, |B| = 8, and |A ∩ B| = 3, what is |A ∪ B|?', '15', '18', '21', '13', '15', 'Inclusion-exclusion: |A ∪ B| = |A| + |B| - |A ∩ B| = 10 + 8 - 3 = 15.', 780, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which of the following is the complement of A ∪ B (relative to universal set U)?', 'A'' ∩ B''', 'A'' ∪ B''', 'A ∩ B', 'A ∪ B''', 'A'' ∩ B''', 'De Morgan''s law: (A ∪ B)'' = A'' ∩ B''. The complement of a union is the intersection of complements.', 820, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A relation R on set A is an equivalence relation if it is reflexive, symmetric, and which third property?', 'Transitive', 'Antisymmetric', 'Asymmetric', 'Total', 'Transitive', 'An equivalence relation requires reflexivity (aRa), symmetry (aRb => bRa), and transitivity (aRb and bRc => aRc).', 900, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The Cartesian product A × B of sets A = {1,2} and B = {a,b} has how many elements?', '4', '2', '6', '8', '4', 'A × B contains all ordered pairs (a,b) with a in A, b in B. |A × B| = |A| * |B| = 2 * 2 = 4.', 740, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── LOGIC – PROPOSITIONAL (6) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
('Which logical connective produces TRUE only when both operands are TRUE?', 'AND (conjunction)', 'OR (disjunction)', 'NOT (negation)', 'XOR (exclusive or)', 'AND (conjunction)', 'The conjunction P ∧ Q is TRUE exactly when both P and Q are TRUE; any FALSE operand makes it FALSE.', 710, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What is the contrapositive of the statement "If P then Q"?', 'If not Q then not P', 'If Q then P', 'If not P then not Q', 'Not P or Q', 'If not Q then not P', 'The contrapositive of P => Q is ¬Q => ¬P, and it is logically equivalent to the original statement.', 800, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which of the following is a tautology?', 'P ∨ ¬P', 'P ∧ ¬P', 'P => P ∧ Q', 'P => Q', 'P ∨ ¬P', 'P ∨ ¬P is the law of excluded middle — it is TRUE for every truth-value assignment, making it a tautology.', 760, 'DiscreteMath'),
('In a truth table for P => Q, which row has the value FALSE?', 'P = TRUE, Q = FALSE', 'P = FALSE, Q = TRUE', 'P = FALSE, Q = FALSE', 'P = TRUE, Q = TRUE', 'P = TRUE, Q = FALSE', 'An implication P => Q is FALSE only when the hypothesis P is TRUE and the conclusion Q is FALSE.', 820, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which equivalence is known as the Distributive Law in propositional logic?', 'P ∧ (Q ∨ R) <=> (P ∧ Q) ∨ (P ∧ R)', 'P ∧ (Q ∧ R) <=> (P ∧ Q) ∧ R', '¬(P ∧ Q) <=> ¬P ∨ ¬Q', 'P ∨ (P ∧ Q) <=> P', 'P ∧ (Q ∨ R) <=> (P ∧ Q) ∨ (P ∧ R)', 'The distributive law states that AND distributes over OR: P ∧ (Q ∨ R) <=> (P ∧ Q) ∨ (P ∧ R).', 880, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What does the universal quantifier ∀x P(x) mean?', 'P(x) is true for every x in the domain', 'There exists at least one x for which P(x) is true', 'P(x) is true for exactly one x', 'P(x) is sometimes true', 'P(x) is true for every x in the domain', '∀x P(x) asserts that the predicate P holds for all elements x in the given domain.', 790, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── PROOF TECHNIQUES (4) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('In a proof by contradiction, you assume what to begin?', 'The negation of what you want to prove', 'The statement you want to prove', 'An arbitrary hypothesis', 'The base case', 'The negation of what you want to prove', 'Proof by contradiction (reductio ad absurdum) assumes ¬P and derives a logical contradiction, proving P must be true.', 850, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What does the inductive step in mathematical induction prove?', 'If the statement holds for k, it holds for k+1', 'The statement holds for k = 0', 'The statement holds for all even integers', 'The statement has a counterexample', 'If the statement holds for k, it holds for k+1', 'The inductive step establishes P(k) => P(k+1). Together with the base case this implies P(n) for all n >= base.', 870, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Prove that sqrt(2) is irrational. Which method is typically used?', 'Proof by contradiction', 'Proof by exhaustion', 'Proof by construction', 'Direct proof', 'Proof by contradiction', 'Assume sqrt(2) = p/q in lowest terms, square both sides to get 2q^2 = p^2, derive that both p and q are even — contradicting lowest terms.', 1050, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Strong induction differs from regular induction in that the inductive hypothesis assumes what?', 'The statement holds for all values up to k', 'The statement holds only for k', 'The statement holds for k-1 only', 'The statement holds for the base case only', 'The statement holds for all values up to k', 'Strong (complete) induction assumes P(1), P(2), ..., P(k) are all true before proving P(k+1), giving more power when needed.', 1100, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── COMBINATORICS (8) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('In how many ways can 5 distinct books be arranged on a shelf?', '120', '60', '25', '24', '120', 'This is a permutation of 5 objects: 5! = 5 * 4 * 3 * 2 * 1 = 120.', 750, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What does C(n, k) = C(n, n-k) illustrate?', 'Symmetry of binomial coefficients', 'Pascal''s identity', 'The binomial theorem', 'Stars and bars', 'Symmetry of binomial coefficients', 'Choosing k items from n is the same as leaving out n-k items, so C(n,k) = C(n,n-k) — the symmetry property.', 810, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The Pigeonhole Principle states that if n+1 items are placed in n containers, at least one container holds how many items?', 'At least 2', 'At most 2', 'Exactly 2', 'At least n', 'At least 2', 'Pigeonhole: if more items than containers, some container must hold at least 2 items. Formally: at least ceil((n+1)/n) = 2.', 870, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What is the coefficient of x^3 * y^2 in the expansion of (x + y)^5?', '10', '20', '5', '15', '10', 'By the binomial theorem, the coefficient of x^3 y^2 in (x+y)^5 is C(5,2) = C(5,3) = 10.', 950, 'DiscreteMath'),
('How many 3-digit numbers can be formed using digits 1-9 if repetition is allowed?', '729', '504', '84', '512', '729', 'Each of the 3 positions has 9 choices (1-9), so the total is 9^3 = 729.', 780, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Stars and bars gives the number of ways to distribute k identical items into n distinct bins (allowing empty bins) as?', 'C(k+n-1, n-1)', 'C(k, n)', 'n^k', 'k! / n!', 'C(k+n-1, n-1)', 'The stars-and-bars formula counts weak compositions: C(k+n-1, n-1) ways to put k identical items into n bins.', 1150, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A committee of 4 is chosen from 6 men and 5 women. How many committees have exactly 2 women?', '150', '60', '90', '120', '150', 'Choose 2 women from 5: C(5,2)=10; choose 2 men from 6: C(6,2)=15; by multiplication rule total = 10*15 = 150.', 1050, 'DiscreteMath'),
('How many strings of length 4 over the alphabet {0, 1} have exactly two 1s?', '6', '4', '8', '12', '6', 'Choose 2 positions out of 4 for the 1s: C(4,2) = 6. The remaining positions are 0.', 880, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── RECURRENCE RELATIONS (3) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
('What is the closed-form solution to the recurrence T(n) = 2*T(n-1) with T(0) = 1?', '2^n', 'n^2', '2n', 'n!', '2^n', 'Unrolling: T(n) = 2*T(n-1) = 2^2*T(n-2) = ... = 2^n*T(0) = 2^n.', 1000, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The recurrence a(n) = a(n-1) + a(n-2) with a(0)=0 and a(1)=1 generates which sequence?', 'Fibonacci sequence', 'Arithmetic sequence', 'Geometric sequence', 'Lucas numbers', 'Fibonacci sequence', 'Starting from 0, 1, each term is the sum of the two before: 0,1,1,2,3,5,8,... — the Fibonacci sequence.', 870, 'DiscreteMath'),
('By the Master Theorem, T(n) = 4*T(n/2) + n has solution?', 'O(n^2)', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n)', 'O(n^3)', 'O(n^2)', 'Here a=4, b=2, f(n)=n. log_b(a)=log_2(4)=2. Since f(n) = O(n^(2-1)), Case 1 applies: T(n) = Theta(n^2).', 1500, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── NUMBER THEORY (5) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('What is 17 mod 5?', '2', '3', '1', '4', '2', '17 = 3*5 + 2, so 17 mod 5 = 2.', 720, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What is gcd(48, 18) computed by the Euclidean algorithm?', '6', '9', '3', '12', '6', '48 = 2*18+12; 18 = 1*12+6; 12 = 2*6+0. The last non-zero remainder is gcd = 6.', 850, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Fermat''s Little Theorem states that if p is prime and gcd(a,p)=1, then?', 'a^(p-1) ≡ 1 (mod p)', 'a^p ≡ a (mod p-1)', 'a^p ≡ 0 (mod p)', 'a^2 ≡ 1 (mod p)', 'a^(p-1) ≡ 1 (mod p)', 'Fermat''s Little Theorem: for prime p and a not divisible by p, a^(p-1) ≡ 1 (mod p). Used in RSA and primality tests.', 1300, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which of the following is NOT a prime number?', '91', '97', '89', '83', '91', '91 = 7 * 13, so it is composite. 83, 89, and 97 are all prime.', 900, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The equation ax ≡ b (mod n) has a solution if and only if what condition holds?', 'gcd(a, n) divides b', 'a divides b', 'n divides b', 'gcd(a, b) = 1', 'gcd(a, n) divides b', 'Linear congruence ax ≡ b (mod n) is solvable iff gcd(a,n) | b, by the theory of Bezout identities.', 1250, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── RELATIONS & FUNCTIONS (5) ────────────────────────────────────────────────
('A function f: A -> B is injective (one-to-one) if?', 'f(x1) = f(x2) implies x1 = x2', 'Every element of B is mapped to', 'f is its own inverse', 'f maps each x to exactly one y', 'f(x1) = f(x2) implies x1 = x2', 'Injectivity: distinct inputs give distinct outputs. f(x1)=f(x2) => x1=x2 (equivalently, no two elements share an image).', 870, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A function f: A -> B is surjective (onto) if?', 'Every element of B has at least one pre-image in A', 'No two elements of A map to the same element of B', 'f has an inverse', 'A and B have the same cardinality', 'Every element of B has at least one pre-image in A', 'Surjectivity means the range equals the codomain B — for every b in B there exists a in A with f(a)=b.', 890, 'DiscreteMath'),
('How many different functions exist from a 3-element set to a 2-element set?', '8', '6', '9', '3', '8', 'Each of the 3 elements of the domain can independently map to any of 2 elements: 2^3 = 8 functions.', 920, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which of the following relations on {1,2,3} is a partial order?', 'R = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,3),(1,3)}', 'R = {(1,2),(2,1)}', 'R = {(1,2),(2,3),(3,1)}', 'R = {(1,2),(1,3)}', 'R = {(1,1),(2,2),(3,3),(1,2),(2,3),(1,3)}', 'A partial order is reflexive, antisymmetric, and transitive. The relation given includes the diagonal (reflexive) and satisfies the other conditions.', 1100, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What is the composition g ∘ f (x) if f(x) = x+1 and g(x) = 2x?', '2x + 2', '2x + 1', 'x + 2', '2x', '2x + 2', 'g ∘ f means apply f first, then g: g(f(x)) = g(x+1) = 2(x+1) = 2x+2.', 830, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── GRAPH THEORY (8) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('What is the degree of a vertex in a graph?', 'The number of edges incident to it', 'The number of vertices connected to it by paths', 'Its label in the graph', 'Its distance from vertex 1', 'The number of edges incident to it', 'The degree of a vertex is the count of edges touching it. A loop contributes 2 to the degree.', 730, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The Handshaking Lemma states that for any graph, the sum of all vertex degrees equals?', '2 * |E|', '|E|', '|V| * |E|', '|V|^2', '2 * |E|', 'Every edge contributes 1 to each of its two endpoint degrees, so the total degree sum = 2|E|.', 820, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A tree with n vertices has exactly how many edges?', 'n - 1', 'n', 'n + 1', 'n * (n-1) / 2', 'n - 1', 'A tree is a connected acyclic graph. By the tree property, every tree on n vertices has exactly n-1 edges.', 860, 'DiscreteMath'),
('An Euler circuit in a connected graph exists if and only if every vertex has what property?', 'Even degree', 'Odd degree', 'Degree at least 2', 'Degree equal to |V|-1', 'Even degree', 'Euler''s theorem: a connected graph has an Euler circuit (traversing every edge exactly once) iff every vertex has even degree.', 1050, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A Hamiltonian path visits every vertex exactly once. What is the complexity of deciding if one exists?', 'NP-complete', 'O(n log n)', 'O(n^2)', 'Polynomial', 'NP-complete', 'The Hamiltonian path/cycle problem is NP-complete; no polynomial-time algorithm is known, unlike the Eulerian case.', 1450, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What is the minimum number of colors needed to properly color the vertices of a cycle C5 (5-cycle)?', '3', '2', '4', '5', '3', 'C5 is an odd cycle. Odd cycles require 3 colors because you cannot 2-color them without a conflict at the last edge.', 1100, 'DiscreteMath'),
('By Kuratowski''s theorem, a graph is planar if and only if it contains no subdivision of K5 or?', 'K3,3', 'K4', 'K6', 'K2,3', 'K3,3', 'Kuratowski''s theorem: a graph is planar iff it has no subgraph homeomorphic to K5 (complete on 5) or K3,3 (complete bipartite 3-3).', 1550, 'DiscreteMath'),
('In a bipartite graph, vertices can be divided into two sets such that every edge connects?', 'A vertex in one set to a vertex in the other set', 'Two vertices in the same set', 'Two vertices of equal degree', 'Adjacent vertices only', 'A vertex in one set to a vertex in the other set', 'A bipartite graph has vertex set V = X ∪ Y (disjoint) and every edge goes from X to Y — no edges within X or within Y.', 900, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── BOOLEAN ALGEBRA (4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('In Boolean algebra, the identity element for the AND operation is?', '1', '0', 'The variable itself', 'Its complement', '1', 'x AND 1 = x for all x. So 1 is the identity for AND, just as 0 is the identity for OR (x OR 0 = x).', 760, 'DiscreteMath'),
('Which Boolean law states that x AND (x OR y) = x?', 'Absorption law', 'Distributive law', 'De Morgan''s law', 'Associative law', 'Absorption law', 'The absorption law: x ∧ (x ∨ y) = x and x ∨ (x ∧ y) = x. The larger expression is "absorbed" by the simpler one.', 1000, 'DiscreteMath'),
('A Boolean function of n variables has how many possible input combinations?', '2^n', 'n^2', '2n', 'n!', '2^n', 'Each of n variables can be 0 or 1 independently, giving 2 * 2 * ... * 2 (n times) = 2^n possible inputs.', 810, 'DiscreteMath'),
('The NAND gate is called a universal gate because?', 'Any Boolean function can be built using only NAND gates', 'It is the fastest gate', 'It has the lowest power consumption', 'It implements both AND and OR directly', 'Any Boolean function can be built using only NAND gates', 'NAND is functionally complete: NOT, AND, and OR can each be expressed in NAND, so all Boolean functions can be realized.', 1150, 'DiscreteMath'),
-- ── COMPLEXITY & MISC (2) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('The class P consists of problems solvable in?', 'Polynomial time by a deterministic Turing machine', 'Polynomial time by a non-deterministic machine', 'Exponential time', 'Logarithmic space', 'Polynomial time by a deterministic Turing machine', 'P is the class of decision problems solvable in O(n^k) time for some constant k on a deterministic Turing machine.', 1200, 'DiscreteMath'),
('What does it mean when we say a binary relation R is antisymmetric?', 'If aRb and bRa then a = b', 'aRb implies bRa', 'aRb implies NOT bRa', 'R contains no pair (a,a)', 'If aRb and bRa then a = b', 'Antisymmetry: if both aRb and bRa hold then a must equal b. This is a key property of partial orders (e.g., <=).', 970, 'DiscreteMath');




-- ── DESCRIPTIVE STATISTICS (easy/medium) ─────────────────────────────────────
('What is the mean of the data set {3, 7, 7, 19}?', '7', '9', '8', '36', '9', 'The mean is the sum (3+7+7+19=36) divided by the count (4), giving 9.', 720, 'Statistics'),
('What is the median of the ordered data set {4, 8, 15, 16, 23}?', '8', '15', '16', '13.2', '15', 'The median is the middle value of an odd-sized sorted list, which is the 3rd element: 15.', 720, 'Statistics'),
('What is the mode of {2, 3, 3, 5, 7, 3, 8}?', '2', '5', '3', '7', '3', 'The mode is the value that appears most frequently; 3 appears three times.', 720, 'Statistics'),
('The variance of {2, 4, 4, 4, 5, 5, 7, 9} is 4. What is the standard deviation?', '16', '2', '8', '4', '2', 'Standard deviation is the square root of the variance: sqrt(4) = 2.', 750, 'Statistics'),
('Which measure of central tendency is most resistant to extreme outliers?', 'Mean', 'Median', 'Range', 'Variance', 'Median', 'The median depends only on the middle ranked value, so outliers have little effect on it.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('For a perfectly symmetric, bell-shaped distribution, which statement is true?', 'Mean > Median > Mode', 'Mean < Median < Mode', 'Mean = Median = Mode', 'Median = 0', 'Mean = Median = Mode', 'In a symmetric unimodal distribution the mean, median, and mode all coincide at the center.', 700, 'Statistics'),
('If every value in a data set is multiplied by a constant k, the standard deviation is multiplied by:', 'k^2', 'sqrt(k)', 'k', '1/k', 'k', 'Multiplying all values by k scales all deviations by k, so sigma is multiplied by |k|.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('The interquartile range (IQR) is defined as:', 'Q3 - Q1', 'Q4 - Q0', 'Q2 - Q1', 'max - min', 'Q3 - Q1', 'IQR = Q3 minus Q1 and measures the spread of the middle 50% of the data.', 720, 'Statistics'),
('A distribution with a long right tail is described as:', 'Negatively skewed', 'Symmetric', 'Positively skewed', 'Bimodal', 'Positively skewed', 'A right (positive) skew means the tail extends toward higher values pulling the mean above the median.', 780, 'Statistics'),
-- ── PROBABILITY RULES (easy/medium) ──────────────────────────────────────────
('Two fair dice are rolled. What is the probability that the sum equals 7?', '1/12', '1/6', '1/9', '7/36', '1/6', 'There are 6 favorable outcomes out of 36 equally likely outcomes, giving 6/36 = 1/6.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('If P(A) = 0.4 and P(B) = 0.5 and A and B are mutually exclusive, what is P(A or B)?', '0.2', '0.9', '0.7', '0.1', '0.9', 'For mutually exclusive events P(A union B) = P(A) + P(B) = 0.4 + 0.5 = 0.9.', 750, 'Statistics'),
('If P(A) = 0.6 and P(B) = 0.5 and P(A and B) = 0.3, what is P(A or B)?', '0.8', '1.1', '0.3', '0.6', '0.8', 'By the addition rule P(A or B) = 0.6 + 0.5 - 0.3 = 0.8.', 780, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CONDITIONAL PROBABILITY & INDEPENDENCE ────────────────────────────────────
('If P(A) = 0.5, P(B) = 0.4, and P(A and B) = 0.2, what is P(A|B)?', '0.4', '0.5', '0.8', '0.2', '0.5', 'P(A|B) = P(A and B) / P(B) = 0.2 / 0.4 = 0.5.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('Events A and B are independent if and only if:', 'P(A and B) = P(A) + P(B)', 'P(A|B) = P(B)', 'P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B)', 'P(A or B) = 0', 'P(A and B) = P(A) * P(B)', 'Independence means the joint probability equals the product of the marginal probabilities.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('A bag has 4 red and 6 blue balls. Two balls are drawn without replacement. What is the probability both are red?', '4/25', '2/15', '12/100', '1/6', '2/15', 'P(both red) = (4/10)*(3/9) = 12/90 = 2/15.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── BAYES'' THEOREM ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('A disease affects 1% of the population. A test is 95% sensitive and 90% specific. Given a positive test, what is the approximate probability of having the disease?', '95%', '1%', '8.7%', '50%', '8.7%', 'By Bayes'' theorem: P(D|+) = (0.95*0.01)/[(0.95*0.01)+(0.10*0.99)] ≈ 0.087, i.e. about 8.7%.', 1750, 'Statistics'),
('Bayes'' theorem states that P(H|E) equals:', 'P(E|H) / P(H)', 'P(H) * P(E)', 'P(E|H) * P(H) / P(E)', 'P(H|E) * P(E) / P(H)', 'P(E|H) * P(H) / P(E)', 'Bayes'' theorem: P(H|E) = P(E|H)*P(H) / P(E), combining the likelihood, prior, and evidence.', 1350, 'Statistics'),
-- ── COMBINATORICS IN PROBABILITY ─────────────────────────────────────────────
('How many ways can 3 students be chosen from a group of 10?', '30', '120', '720', '10', '120', 'The number of combinations is C(10,3) = 10!/(3!*7!) = 120.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('A committee of 2 men and 2 women is chosen from 5 men and 4 women. How many committees are possible?', '40', '60', '20', '120', '60', 'C(5,2)*C(4,2) = 10*6 = 60 possible committees.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── DISCRETE DISTRIBUTIONS – BINOMIAL ────────────────────────────────────────
('A fair coin is flipped 6 times. What is the probability of exactly 2 heads?', '15/64', '1/4', '6/64', '1/16', '15/64', 'P(X=2) = C(6,2)*(0.5)^2*(0.5)^4 = 15*(1/64) = 15/64.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
('For a binomial distribution B(n, p), the mean is:', 'n/p', 'n*p*(1-p)', 'np', 'sqrt(np)', 'np', 'The expected value of a binomial random variable is E[X] = np.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('For a binomial distribution B(n, p), the variance is:', 'np', 'np^2', 'np(1-p)', 'n^2*p', 'np(1-p)', 'The variance of a binomial random variable is Var(X) = np(1-p).', 950, 'Statistics'),
('Which condition is NOT required for a binomial distribution?', 'Fixed number of trials', 'Each trial is independent', 'Continuous outcomes', 'Constant probability of success', 'Continuous outcomes', 'Binomial trials must have binary (not continuous) outcomes: success or failure.', 780, 'Statistics'),
-- ── DISCRETE DISTRIBUTIONS – POISSON ─────────────────────────────────────────
('The Poisson distribution with parameter lambda has mean equal to:', 'lambda^2', '1/lambda', 'sqrt(lambda)', 'lambda', 'lambda', 'For a Poisson distribution both the mean and variance equal lambda.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('A call center receives an average of 4 calls per minute. What is the probability of receiving exactly 0 calls in a given minute? (e ≈ 2.718)', 'e^(-4)', '4*e^(-4)', '1 - e^(-4)', '4/e', 'e^(-4)', 'P(X=0) = e^(-4)*4^0/0! = e^(-4) ≈ 0.0183.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
('For a Poisson random variable with lambda = 3, the variance is:', '9', 'sqrt(3)', '6', '3', '3', 'The variance of a Poisson distribution equals its parameter lambda, so Var(X) = 3.', 950, 'Statistics'),
-- ── DISCRETE DISTRIBUTIONS – GEOMETRIC ───────────────────────────────────────
('A geometric distribution models:', 'The number of successes in n trials', 'The number of trials until the first success', 'The number of defects per unit area', 'The waiting time between events', 'The number of trials until the first success', 'The geometric distribution counts trials needed to obtain the first success in Bernoulli trials.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('For a geometric distribution with success probability p, the mean number of trials until the first success is:', 'p', '1-p', '1/p', 'p/(1-p)', '1/p', 'E[X] = 1/p for the geometric distribution counting the trial of first success.', 950, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CONTINUOUS DISTRIBUTIONS – UNIFORM ───────────────────────────────────────
('For a continuous uniform distribution on [a, b], the mean is:', '(b-a)/2', 'a + b', 'b - a', '(a+b)/2', '(a+b)/2', 'The mean of U(a,b) is the midpoint of the interval: (a+b)/2.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('For a continuous uniform distribution on [2, 8], what is the variance?', '1', '3', '36', '6', '3', 'Var(X) = (b-a)^2/12 = (8-2)^2/12 = 36/12 = 3.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CONTINUOUS DISTRIBUTIONS – NORMAL ────────────────────────────────────────
('A standard normal distribution has mean mu and standard deviation sigma equal to:', 'mu=1, sigma=0', 'mu=0, sigma=0', 'mu=1, sigma=1', 'mu=0, sigma=1', 'mu=0, sigma=1', 'The standard normal Z ~ N(0,1) has mean 0 and standard deviation 1 by definition.', 700, 'Statistics'),
('If X ~ N(50, 100) (mean=50, variance=100), what is the z-score for X=65?', '0.15', '1.5', '65', '0.65', '1.5', 'z = (X - mu)/sigma = (65 - 50)/10 = 1.5, where sigma = sqrt(100) = 10.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('The empirical rule states that approximately what percentage of data falls within 2 standard deviations of the mean in a normal distribution?', '68%', '99.7%', '95%', '75%', '95%', 'The 68-95-99.7 rule states ~95% of data lies within 2 sigma of the mean.', 750, 'Statistics'),
('Which property is NOT true of the normal distribution?', 'It is symmetric about the mean', 'The total area under the curve equals 1', 'It has a fixed range [0,1]', 'Mean = Median = Mode', 'It has a fixed range [0,1]', 'The normal distribution is defined over all real numbers, not bounded between 0 and 1.', 780, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CONTINUOUS DISTRIBUTIONS – EXPONENTIAL ───────────────────────────────────
('The exponential distribution with rate parameter lambda has mean equal to:', 'lambda', 'lambda^2', '1/lambda', 'sqrt(lambda)', '1/lambda', 'For an exponential distribution the mean (expected value) is 1/lambda.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('The memoryless property is a characteristic of which distribution?', 'Normal', 'Binomial', 'Exponential', 'Uniform', 'Exponential', 'The exponential distribution is the only continuous distribution with the memoryless property.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── EXPECTATION & VARIANCE ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('If E[X] = 3 and Var(X) = 4, what is E[2X + 1]?', '7', '10', '9', '8', '7', 'E[2X+1] = 2*E[X]+1 = 2*3+1 = 7 by the linearity of expectation.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('If X and Y are independent, Var(X + Y) equals:', 'Var(X) - Var(Y)', 'Var(X) * Var(Y)', 'Var(X) + Var(Y)', '(Var(X) + Var(Y))^2', 'Var(X) + Var(Y)', 'For independent random variables, variances are additive: Var(X+Y) = Var(X) + Var(Y).', 1100, 'Statistics'),
('If Var(X) = 9, what is Var(3X - 5)?', '27', '72', '81', '9', '81', 'Var(aX + b) = a^2*Var(X), so Var(3X-5) = 9*9 = 81.', 1350, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CENTRAL LIMIT THEOREM & SAMPLING ─────────────────────────────────────────
('The Central Limit Theorem states that for large n, the sample mean X-bar is approximately:', 'Exponentially distributed', 'Chi-squared distributed', 'Normally distributed', 'Uniformly distributed', 'Normally distributed', 'The CLT guarantees that the sampling distribution of X-bar approaches a normal distribution as n increases, regardless of the population distribution.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('According to the CLT, the standard error of the sample mean is:', 'sigma * n', 'sigma / n', 'sigma / sqrt(n)', 'sigma^2 / n', 'sigma / sqrt(n)', 'The standard error (standard deviation of X-bar) is sigma divided by sqrt(n).', 950, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CONFIDENCE INTERVALS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('A 95% confidence interval for mu means:', 'P(mu is in the interval) = 0.95', 'There is a 95% chance mu equals X-bar', 'In repeated sampling, 95% of such intervals will contain mu', 'The interval contains 95% of individual data points', 'In repeated sampling, 95% of such intervals will contain mu', 'A confidence interval is a frequentist statement about the procedure: 95% of CIs constructed this way cover the true parameter.', 1350, 'Statistics'),
('For a large sample with known sigma, a 99% confidence interval for the mean uses z* =', '1.645', '1.96', '2.576', '2.33', '2.576', 'The z-critical value for a two-tailed 99% CI is z* = 2.576, corresponding to 0.5% in each tail.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── HYPOTHESIS TESTING ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('A Type I error occurs when:', 'H0 is true and we fail to reject it', 'H0 is false and we reject it', 'H0 is true and we reject it', 'H0 is false and we fail to reject it', 'H0 is true and we reject it', 'A Type I error (false positive) is rejecting a true null hypothesis; its probability is the significance level alpha.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
('A Type II error occurs when:', 'H0 is false and we reject it', 'H0 is true and we reject it', 'H0 is false and we fail to reject it', 'H0 is true and we fail to reject it', 'H0 is false and we fail to reject it', 'A Type II error (false negative) is failing to reject a false null hypothesis; its probability is denoted beta.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
('If the p-value of a hypothesis test is 0.03 and the significance level is alpha = 0.05, what do you conclude?', 'Fail to reject H0; insufficient evidence', 'Reject H0; statistically significant result', 'Accept H0; the result is conclusive', 'Reject H1; result is not significant', 'Reject H0; statistically significant result', 'Since p-value (0.03) < alpha (0.05), we reject the null hypothesis at the 5% significance level.', 950, 'Statistics'),
('A chi-square test for goodness of fit is used to:', 'Compare two population means', 'Test whether observed frequencies match expected frequencies', 'Estimate the population standard deviation', 'Test whether a distribution is normal only', 'Test whether observed frequencies match expected frequencies', 'The chi-square goodness-of-fit test assesses how well observed counts match a hypothesized distribution.', 1100, 'Statistics'),
-- ── CORRELATION & REGRESSION ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
('A correlation coefficient r = -0.92 indicates:', 'No linear relationship', 'Weak positive linear relationship', 'Strong negative linear relationship', 'Perfect positive linear relationship', 'Strong negative linear relationship', 'r close to -1 indicates a strong negative (inverse) linear relationship between the two variables.', 780, 'Statistics'),
('In simple linear regression y = b0 + b1*x, b1 represents:', 'The y-intercept', 'The predicted value of y when x = 0', 'The change in y for a one-unit increase in x', 'The correlation coefficient', 'The change in y for a one-unit increase in x', 'The slope b1 is the estimated change in the response y for each additional unit of the predictor x.', 950, 'Statistics'),
-- ── MAXIMUM LIKELIHOOD ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
('The maximum likelihood estimator (MLE) of the mean mu for a normal population N(mu, sigma^2) with a random sample x1,...,xn is:', 'The sample median', 'sigma/sqrt(n)', 'The sample mean x-bar', 'The sample mode', 'The sample mean x-bar', 'Maximizing the normal log-likelihood with respect to mu yields the sample mean as the MLE.', 1600, 'Statistics');


-- SKELETAL SYSTEM (Bones & Joints) --
('How many bones are in the adult human body?', '206', '208', '212', '196', '206', 'The adult human skeleton consists of 206 bones, reduced from approximately 270 in infancy through fusion.', 750, 'Anatomy'),
('Which carpal bone is most commonly fractured?', 'Scaphoid', 'Lunate', 'Trapezium', 'Hamate', 'Scaphoid', 'The scaphoid is the most frequently fractured carpal bone, typically from a fall on an outstretched hand.', 980, 'Anatomy'),
('What type of joint is the hip joint?', 'Ball-and-socket joint', 'Hinge joint', 'Saddle joint', 'Pivot joint', 'Ball-and-socket joint', 'The hip joint is a ball-and-socket synovial joint, allowing multiaxial movement between the femoral head and the acetabulum.', 730, 'Anatomy'),
('Which bone forms the posterior part of the hard palate?', 'Palatine bone', 'Maxilla', 'Vomer', 'Sphenoid bone', 'Palatine bone', 'The horizontal plates of the paired palatine bones form the posterior one-third of the hard palate.', 1050, 'Anatomy'),
('The olecranon process is located on which bone?', 'Ulna', 'Radius', 'Humerus', 'Scapula', 'Ulna', 'The olecranon is the bony projection at the proximal end of the ulna that forms the point of the elbow.', 960, 'Anatomy'),
('Which suture joins the frontal and parietal bones?', 'Coronal suture', 'Sagittal suture', 'Lambdoid suture', 'Squamous suture', 'Coronal suture', 'The coronal suture is a fibrous joint that runs transversely across the skull separating the frontal bone from the two parietal bones.', 1100, 'Anatomy'),
-- MUSCULAR SYSTEM --
('Which muscle is responsible for plantarflexion and is nicknamed the "toe dancer''s muscle"?', 'Gastrocnemius', 'Tibialis anterior', 'Peroneus longus', 'Soleus', 'Gastrocnemius', 'The gastrocnemius is the large, superficial calf muscle primarily responsible for plantarflexion when the knee is extended.', 800, 'Anatomy'),
('The rotator cuff is composed of four muscles; which of the following is NOT one of them?', 'Teres major', 'Supraspinatus', 'Infraspinatus', 'Subscapularis', 'Teres major', 'The rotator cuff consists of supraspinatus, infraspinatus, teres minor, and subscapularis; teres major is a separate muscle.', 1200, 'Anatomy'),
('Which muscle is the primary flexor of the forearm at the elbow?', 'Brachialis', 'Biceps brachii', 'Brachioradialis', 'Coracobrachialis', 'Brachialis', 'The brachialis lies deep to the biceps brachii and is the most powerful flexor of the elbow regardless of forearm position.', 1300, 'Anatomy'),
('What is the origin of the deltoid muscle?', 'Clavicle, acromion, and spine of scapula', 'Clavicle and coracoid process', 'Acromion and coracoid process', 'Greater tubercle of humerus', 'Clavicle, acromion, and spine of scapula', 'The deltoid originates from the lateral third of the clavicle, the acromion, and the spine of the scapula.', 1150, 'Anatomy'),
('Which muscle forms the bulk of the anterior abdominal wall and is referred to as the "six-pack" muscle?', 'Rectus abdominis', 'External oblique', 'Internal oblique', 'Transversus abdominis', 'Rectus abdominis', 'The rectus abdominis is the paired, segmented vertical muscle of the anterior abdomen responsible for trunk flexion.', 710, 'Anatomy'),
-- HEART & CIRCULATORY ANATOMY --
('Which valve separates the left atrium from the left ventricle?', 'Mitral valve', 'Tricuspid valve', 'Aortic valve', 'Pulmonary valve', 'Mitral valve', 'The mitral (bicuspid) valve has two leaflets and controls blood flow from the left atrium into the left ventricle.', 760, 'Anatomy'),
('The sinoatrial node, the heart''s primary pacemaker, is located in which chamber?', 'Right atrium', 'Left atrium', 'Right ventricle', 'Left ventricle', 'Right atrium', 'The sinoatrial node is situated in the wall of the right atrium near the opening of the superior vena cava.', 870, 'Anatomy'),
('Which artery supplies the anterior wall of the left ventricle and the interventricular septum?', 'Left anterior descending artery', 'Right coronary artery', 'Left circumflex artery', 'Posterior descending artery', 'Left anterior descending artery', 'The left anterior descending (LAD) artery is a branch of the left coronary artery and is commonly called "the widow maker."', 1020, 'Anatomy'),
('The fossa ovalis is a remnant of which fetal structure?', 'Foramen ovale', 'Ductus arteriosus', 'Ductus venosus', 'Umbilical vein', 'Foramen ovale', 'The fossa ovalis is an oval depression in the interatrial septum representing the closed foramen ovale of fetal circulation.', 1180, 'Anatomy'),
('Which great vessel carries deoxygenated blood from the right ventricle to the lungs?', 'Pulmonary artery', 'Pulmonary vein', 'Aorta', 'Superior vena cava', 'Pulmonary artery', 'The pulmonary artery (pulmonary trunk) is unique in being an artery that carries deoxygenated blood to the lungs for gas exchange.', 720, 'Anatomy'),
-- RESPIRATORY ANATOMY --
('The carina is a ridge of cartilage located at the bifurcation of the trachea into which structures?', 'Left and right main bronchi', 'Left and right secondary bronchi', 'Bronchioles and alveoli', 'Larynx and trachea', 'Left and right main bronchi', 'The carina is the internal keel-like ridge at the level of the sternal angle where the trachea divides into the left and right main (primary) bronchi.', 1250, 'Anatomy'),
('Which lobe of the right lung is separated from the middle lobe by the horizontal fissure?', 'Upper lobe', 'Lower lobe', 'Lingula', 'Cardiac notch', 'Upper lobe', 'The horizontal fissure of the right lung separates the upper lobe from the middle lobe, running at the level of the 4th costal cartilage.', 1050, 'Anatomy'),
('The epiglottis is composed of which type of cartilage?', 'Elastic cartilage', 'Hyaline cartilage', 'Fibrocartilage', 'Calcified cartilage', 'Elastic cartilage', 'The epiglottis is made of elastic cartilage, which allows it to fold down over the glottis during swallowing to prevent aspiration.', 1350, 'Anatomy'),
('What is the functional unit of gas exchange in the lung?', 'Alveolus', 'Bronchiole', 'Respiratory bronchiole', 'Alveolar duct', 'Alveolus', 'The alveolus is the tiny air sac in the lung where oxygen and carbon dioxide are exchanged between air and blood across a thin membrane.', 700, 'Anatomy'),
-- DIGESTIVE ANATOMY --
('The hepatopancreatic ampulla (ampulla of Vater) opens into which part of the small intestine?', 'Second part of duodenum', 'First part of duodenum', 'Jejunum', 'Ileum', 'Second part of duodenum', 'The ampulla of Vater, where the common bile duct and main pancreatic duct unite, opens into the descending (second) part of the duodenum.', 1280, 'Anatomy'),
('Which part of the large intestine lacks taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices?', 'Rectum', 'Sigmoid colon', 'Ascending colon', 'Transverse colon', 'Rectum', 'The rectum lacks the three anatomical features (taeniae coli, haustra, and omental appendices) that characterize the rest of the large intestine.', 1380, 'Anatomy'),
('The liver receives its dual blood supply from the hepatic artery and which other vessel?', 'Portal vein', 'Hepatic vein', 'Inferior vena cava', 'Splenic vein', 'Portal vein', 'The hepatic portal vein delivers nutrient-rich blood from the GI tract to the liver, contributing about 75% of its total blood supply.', 900, 'Anatomy'),
('Which sphincter controls passage of chyme from the stomach into the duodenum?', 'Pyloric sphincter', 'Ileocecal valve', 'Cardiac sphincter', 'Internal anal sphincter', 'Pyloric sphincter', 'The pyloric sphincter is a thickening of the circular smooth muscle at the gastroduodenal junction that regulates gastric emptying.', 760, 'Anatomy'),
-- NERVOUS SYSTEM --
('Which cranial nerve is responsible for the "gag reflex" afferent limb?', 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'Vagus nerve (CN X)', 'Facial nerve (CN VII)', 'Trigeminal nerve (CN V)', 'Glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX)', 'The afferent limb of the gag reflex is carried by the glossopharyngeal nerve (CN IX) from the posterior pharynx.', 1400, 'Anatomy'),
('The Broca''s area, responsible for speech production, is located in which lobe of the brain?', 'Frontal lobe', 'Temporal lobe', 'Parietal lobe', 'Occipital lobe', 'Frontal lobe', 'Broca''s area is located in the inferior frontal gyrus of the dominant frontal lobe and controls the motor production of speech.', 950, 'Anatomy'),
('Which tract carries pain and temperature sensation from the body to the brain?', 'Lateral spinothalamic tract', 'Dorsal columns', 'Corticospinal tract', 'Rubrospinal tract', 'Lateral spinothalamic tract', 'The lateral spinothalamic tract (anterolateral system) carries pain and temperature from the contralateral side of the body to the thalamus.', 1100, 'Anatomy'),
('The cauda equina is formed by nerve roots below which vertebral level?', 'L1', 'L3', 'T12', 'S1', 'L1', 'The spinal cord typically ends at L1-L2 in adults; below this level the nerve roots form the cauda equina (horse''s tail).', 1200, 'Anatomy'),
('Which structure connects the two cerebral hemispheres and is the largest white matter commissure?', 'Corpus callosum', 'Anterior commissure', 'Posterior commissure', 'Fornix', 'Corpus callosum', 'The corpus callosum is the largest commissure in the brain, containing approximately 200 million axons connecting the two cerebral hemispheres.', 800, 'Anatomy'),
('Which vessel is NOT part of the circle of Willis?', 'Vertebral artery', 'Anterior cerebral artery', 'Posterior communicating artery', 'Internal carotid artery', 'Vertebral artery', 'The vertebral arteries join to form the basilar artery, which then contributes to the circle of Willis, but the vertebral arteries themselves are not part of the circle.', 1600, 'Anatomy'),
-- EYE & EAR --
('Which layer of the eye contains the photoreceptors (rods and cones)?', 'Retina', 'Choroid', 'Sclera', 'Ciliary body', 'Retina', 'The retina is the innermost layer of the eye and contains the photoreceptor cells — rods for dim light and cones for color vision.', 740, 'Anatomy'),
('The malleus, incus, and stapes are collectively known as what?', 'Ossicles', 'Cochlear bones', 'Tympanic bones', 'Labyrinthine bones', 'Ossicles', 'The three ossicles (auditory bones) of the middle ear transmit vibrations from the tympanic membrane to the oval window of the inner ear.', 770, 'Anatomy'),
('The aqueous humor is produced by which structure in the eye?', 'Ciliary body', 'Choroid plexus', 'Retinal pigment epithelium', 'Iris', 'Ciliary body', 'Aqueous humor is secreted by the non-pigmented epithelium of the ciliary body and drains through the trabecular meshwork.', 1150, 'Anatomy'),
('Which cranial nerve innervates the lateral rectus muscle of the eye?', 'Abducens nerve (CN VI)', 'Oculomotor nerve (CN III)', 'Trochlear nerve (CN IV)', 'Optic nerve (CN II)', 'Abducens nerve (CN VI)', 'The abducens nerve (CN VI) solely innervates the lateral rectus muscle, which abducts the eye.', 1020, 'Anatomy'),
-- ENDOCRINE GLANDS --
('The islets of Langerhans are found in which organ?', 'Pancreas', 'Liver', 'Spleen', 'Adrenal gland', 'Pancreas', 'The islets of Langerhans are clusters of endocrine cells scattered throughout the pancreas that produce insulin, glucagon, and somatostatin.', 800, 'Anatomy'),
('Which zone of the adrenal cortex produces aldosterone?', 'Zona glomerulosa', 'Zona fasciculata', 'Zona reticularis', 'Adrenal medulla', 'Zona glomerulosa', 'The outermost zona glomerulosa is the exclusive site of aldosterone synthesis, which regulates sodium and potassium balance.', 1380, 'Anatomy'),
('The posterior pituitary (neurohypophysis) stores and releases which hormones?', 'ADH and oxytocin', 'GH and prolactin', 'TSH and ACTH', 'FSH and LH', 'ADH and oxytocin', 'ADH (vasopressin) and oxytocin are synthesized in the hypothalamus and transported to the posterior pituitary for storage and release.', 1050, 'Anatomy'),
('Which gland, located in the mediastinum, is responsible for T-cell maturation?', 'Thymus', 'Thyroid', 'Parathyroid', 'Pineal gland', 'Thymus', 'The thymus is a bilobed gland in the anterior superior mediastinum where immature T-lymphocytes mature and become immunocompetent.', 900, 'Anatomy'),
-- URINARY / RENAL ANATOMY --
('The functional unit of the kidney is called what?', 'Nephron', 'Glomerulus', 'Loop of Henle', 'Collecting duct', 'Nephron', 'The nephron is the structural and functional unit of the kidney, consisting of the glomerulus and renal tubules.', 720, 'Anatomy'),
('The detrusor muscle is found in the wall of which organ?', 'Urinary bladder', 'Ureter', 'Renal pelvis', 'Urethra', 'Urinary bladder', 'The detrusor muscle is the thick smooth muscle layer of the urinary bladder wall that contracts during micturition to expel urine.', 870, 'Anatomy'),
-- REPRODUCTIVE ANATOMY --
('In males, which structure connects the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct?', 'Ductus deferens (vas deferens)', 'Seminal vesicle duct', 'Efferent ductules', 'Prostatic urethra', 'Ductus deferens (vas deferens)', 'The ductus deferens (vas deferens) is a muscular tube that carries sperm from the epididymis to the ejaculatory duct within the prostate.', 1100, 'Anatomy'),
('The uterine tube (fallopian tube) is divided into four parts; which part is widest and longest?', 'Ampulla', 'Isthmus', 'Infundibulum', 'Intramural part', 'Ampulla', 'The ampulla is the longest and widest portion of the uterine tube and is the most common site of fertilization.', 1250, 'Anatomy'),
-- LYMPHATIC SYSTEM --
('The thoracic duct drains lymph from all regions EXCEPT which?', 'Right upper limb and right side of the thorax, head, and neck', 'Lower limbs', 'Left side of the thorax', 'Abdominal viscera', 'Right upper limb and right side of the thorax, head, and neck', 'The right lymphatic duct drains the right upper limb and right head/neck/thorax; the thoracic duct drains everything else.', 1550, 'Anatomy'),
('Lymph from the lower extremities first passes through which lymph node group?', 'Inguinal lymph nodes', 'Popliteal lymph nodes', 'Iliac lymph nodes', 'Para-aortic lymph nodes', 'Inguinal lymph nodes', 'The superficial inguinal lymph nodes are the primary regional nodes receiving lymph from the lower extremities, perineum, and external genitalia.', 950, 'Anatomy'),
-- ANATOMICAL TERMINOLOGY --
('Which anatomical plane divides the body into anterior (front) and posterior (back) halves?', 'Coronal (frontal) plane', 'Sagittal plane', 'Transverse (axial) plane', 'Oblique plane', 'Coronal (frontal) plane', 'The coronal (frontal) plane is a vertical plane that divides the body into anterior and posterior sections.', 700, 'Anatomy'),
('In anatomical terminology, "medial" means closer to what reference point?', 'The midline of the body', 'The surface of the body', 'The head', 'The point of attachment of a limb', 'The midline of the body', 'Medial refers to structures closer to the midline (midsagittal plane) of the body, whereas lateral refers to structures farther from the midline.', 700, 'Anatomy'),
-- HISTOLOGY BASICS --
('Which type of epithelium lines the trachea and is characterized by cells that appear to be in multiple layers but all contact the basement membrane?', 'Pseudostratified columnar epithelium', 'Stratified squamous epithelium', 'Simple columnar epithelium', 'Transitional epithelium', 'Pseudostratified columnar epithelium', 'Pseudostratified ciliated columnar epithelium lines most of the respiratory tract; all cells touch the basement membrane despite their nuclei being at different levels.', 1400, 'Anatomy'),
('Which connective tissue cell type is responsible for producing collagen fibers?', 'Fibroblast', 'Macrophage', 'Mast cell', 'Plasma cell', 'Fibroblast', 'Fibroblasts are the principal cells of connective tissue proper and are responsible for synthesizing collagen, elastin, and ground substance.', 980, 'Anatomy'),
('Transitional epithelium (urothelium) is characteristically found lining which structures?', 'Urinary bladder, ureters, and renal pelvis', 'Small intestine and colon', 'Trachea and bronchi', 'Esophagus and vagina', 'Urinary bladder, ureters, and renal pelvis', 'Transitional epithelium is specialized to stretch and recoil, making it ideal for lining the urinary bladder, ureters, and renal pelvis.', 1100, 'Anatomy');


-- PROKARYOTIC CELL STRUCTURE (easy)
('Which structure is responsible for protein synthesis in prokaryotes?', 'Ribosome', 'Mitochondrion', 'Endoplasmic reticulum', 'Golgi apparatus', 'Ribosome', 'Prokaryotes lack membrane-bound organelles but possess 70S ribosomes that carry out translation.', 800, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the primary function of the bacterial capsule?', 'Protection from phagocytosis', 'DNA replication', 'Energy production', 'Protein secretion', 'Protection from phagocytosis', 'The polysaccharide capsule shields bacteria from host phagocytes, acting as a key virulence factor.', 750, 'Microbiology'),

('Which component of the bacterial cell wall is targeted by penicillin?', 'Peptidoglycan', 'Lipopolysaccharide', 'Teichoic acid', 'Porin proteins', 'Peptidoglycan', 'Penicillin inhibits transpeptidase enzymes that cross-link peptidoglycan strands, weakening the cell wall.', 900, 'Microbiology'),

('What structure allows bacteria to adhere to surfaces and initiate biofilm formation?', 'Fimbriae (pili)', 'Flagella', 'Capsule', 'Endospore', 'Fimbriae (pili)', 'Fimbriae are short, hair-like appendages that mediate initial attachment of bacteria to host cells and abiotic surfaces.', 820, 'Microbiology'),

-- GRAM STAINING (easy/medium)
('In the Gram stain procedure, what is the purpose of the decolorizer step?', 'To wash away crystal violet from Gram-negative cells', 'To fix the smear to the slide', 'To stain Gram-positive cells red', 'To add the counterstain safranin', 'To wash away crystal violet from Gram-negative cells', 'The alcohol/acetone decolorizer dissolves the outer membrane of Gram-negative cells, releasing the crystal violet-iodine complex.', 870, 'Microbiology'),

('A bacterium stains pink after a Gram stain. What does this indicate about its cell wall?', 'It has a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane', 'It has a thick peptidoglycan layer with no outer membrane', 'It lacks a cell wall entirely', 'It contains large amounts of teichoic acid', 'It has a thin peptidoglycan layer and an outer membrane', 'Gram-negative bacteria appear pink because they lose crystal violet during decolorization and absorb safranin counterstain.', 950, 'Microbiology'),

('Which of the following is a Gram-positive bacterium?', 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'Escherichia coli', 'Neisseria gonorrhoeae', 'Salmonella enterica', 'Staphylococcus aureus', 'Staphylococcus aureus has a thick peptidoglycan cell wall that retains crystal violet, making it Gram-positive.', 780, 'Microbiology'),

-- BACTERIAL MORPHOLOGY (easy)
('Which bacterial morphology is described as a chain of spherical cells?', 'Streptococcus', 'Staphylococcus', 'Bacillus', 'Vibrio', 'Streptococcus', 'Streptococci divide in a single plane and remain attached, forming linear chains of cocci.', 720, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the term for the curved, comma-shaped bacterial morphology?', 'Vibrio', 'Spirochete', 'Coccobacillus', 'Spirillum', 'Vibrio', 'Vibrio cholerae is the classic example of a curved rod (vibrio) morphology.', 700, 'Microbiology'),

-- BACTERIAL GROWTH CURVE (medium)
('During which phase of the bacterial growth curve does the population size remain constant because growth equals death?', 'Stationary phase', 'Lag phase', 'Exponential (log) phase', 'Death phase', 'Stationary phase', 'In the stationary phase, nutrient depletion and waste accumulation cause growth rate to equal death rate, stabilising cell numbers.', 1000, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the term for the time required for a bacterial population to double during logarithmic growth?', 'Generation time', 'Doubling constant', 'Growth coefficient', 'Replication interval', 'Generation time', 'Generation time (doubling time) is the period between successive binary fissions under optimal conditions.', 950, 'Microbiology'),

-- MICROBIAL METABOLISM (medium/hard)
('Which metabolic pathway do obligate anaerobes use to generate ATP in the absence of oxygen?', 'Fermentation', 'Oxidative phosphorylation', 'Calvin cycle', 'Beta-oxidation', 'Fermentation', 'Obligate anaerobes rely solely on substrate-level phosphorylation (fermentation) because they cannot use oxygen as a terminal electron acceptor.', 1100, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the final electron acceptor in aerobic respiration?', 'Oxygen', 'NAD+', 'Pyruvate', 'Sulfate', 'Oxygen', 'In aerobic respiration, molecular oxygen accepts electrons at the end of the electron transport chain, forming water.', 800, 'Microbiology'),

('Which enzyme catalyses the conversion of pyruvate to acetyl-CoA, linking glycolysis to the TCA cycle?', 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex', 'Lactate dehydrogenase', 'Phosphofructokinase', 'Isocitrate dehydrogenase', 'Pyruvate dehydrogenase complex', 'The pyruvate dehydrogenase complex oxidatively decarboxylates pyruvate, producing acetyl-CoA, CO2, and NADH.', 1350, 'Microbiology'),

('What type of fermentation produces ethanol and CO2 as end products?', 'Alcoholic fermentation', 'Lactic acid fermentation', 'Mixed acid fermentation', 'Propionic acid fermentation', 'Alcoholic fermentation', 'Yeast and some bacteria reduce pyruvate to ethanol and CO2 to regenerate NAD+ under anaerobic conditions.', 1000, 'Microbiology'),

-- MICROBIAL GENETICS (medium/hard)
('In bacterial transformation, exogenous DNA enters a cell from which source?', 'The surrounding environment', 'A bacteriophage vector', 'Direct cell-to-cell contact', 'A plasmid donor cell', 'The surrounding environment', 'Transformation involves uptake of naked DNA from the environment by naturally or artificially competent bacteria.', 1050, 'Microbiology'),

('Which horizontal gene transfer mechanism requires direct physical contact between donor and recipient bacterial cells?', 'Conjugation', 'Transformation', 'Transduction', 'Transposition', 'Conjugation', 'Conjugation transfers DNA through a pilus-mediated mating junction, requiring cell-to-cell contact.', 1150, 'Microbiology'),

('In generalised transduction, which entity packages random fragments of host DNA into phage capsids?', 'A bacteriophage during assembly', 'A plasmid integrase', 'A restriction enzyme', 'A transposon', 'A bacteriophage during assembly', 'During generalised transduction, a phage mistakenly packages random bacterial DNA instead of its own genome into capsids.', 1400, 'Microbiology'),

('What type of genetic element is an F plasmid in Escherichia coli?', 'Conjugative plasmid encoding a sex pilus', 'Resistance plasmid encoding antibiotic resistance genes', 'Virulence plasmid encoding exotoxins', 'Bacteriocin-encoding plasmid', 'Conjugative plasmid encoding a sex pilus', 'The F (fertility) plasmid carries tra genes that encode the F pilus needed for conjugative mating.', 1500, 'Microbiology'),

('Which mutation type involves the insertion or deletion of a nucleotide, causing a shift in the reading frame?', 'Frameshift mutation', 'Missense mutation', 'Nonsense mutation', 'Silent mutation', 'Frameshift mutation', 'Insertions or deletions of one or two nucleotides shift the triplet reading frame, altering all downstream codons.', 1200, 'Microbiology'),

-- VIRUSES (medium/hard)
('In the lytic cycle of a bacteriophage, what is the immediate outcome after viral replication is complete?', 'Host cell lysis and release of new virions', 'Integration of viral DNA into the host chromosome', 'Host cell enters a dormant state', 'Viral DNA is degraded by host restriction enzymes', 'Host cell lysis and release of new virions', 'The lytic cycle culminates in lysis of the bacterial cell, releasing hundreds of progeny phage particles.', 950, 'Microbiology'),

('What term describes the state in which bacteriophage DNA is integrated into the host chromosome and replicates with it?', 'Lysogeny', 'Lytic infection', 'Transduction', 'Abortive infection', 'Lysogeny', 'In lysogeny, the phage genome (prophage) integrates into and replicates with the bacterial chromosome without causing immediate lysis.', 1100, 'Microbiology'),

('Which viral structural component is responsible for host cell receptor binding in animal viruses?', 'Envelope glycoproteins (spikes)', 'Capsid proteins', 'Matrix proteins', 'Polymerase complex', 'Envelope glycoproteins (spikes)', 'Surface glycoproteins on the viral envelope recognise and bind specific host cell receptors, initiating infection.', 1250, 'Microbiology'),

('What type of viral genome does HIV (Human Immunodeficiency Virus) possess?', 'Single-stranded positive-sense RNA', 'Double-stranded DNA', 'Single-stranded negative-sense RNA', 'Double-stranded RNA', 'Single-stranded positive-sense RNA', 'HIV is a retrovirus with a single-stranded (+)RNA genome that is reverse-transcribed into double-stranded DNA and integrated into the host genome.', 1450, 'Microbiology'),

('Which enzyme is unique to retroviruses and converts their RNA genome into DNA?', 'Reverse transcriptase', 'RNA polymerase', 'DNA ligase', 'Integrase', 'Reverse transcriptase', 'Reverse transcriptase is an RNA-dependent DNA polymerase encoded by retroviruses to convert their RNA genome into proviral DNA.', 1300, 'Microbiology'),

-- FUNGI & PROTOZOA BASICS (easy/medium)
('Which fungal cell wall component is absent in mammalian cells, making it a target for antifungal drugs?', 'Ergosterol', 'Cholesterol', 'Cellulose', 'Chitin', 'Ergosterol', 'Fungal membranes contain ergosterol instead of cholesterol; antifungals like amphotericin B and azoles exploit this difference.', 1050, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the asexual spore produced by Aspergillus species called?', 'Conidium', 'Ascospore', 'Basidiospore', 'Zygospore', 'Conidium', 'Aspergillus reproduces asexually by producing conidia (conidiospores) on specialised structures called conidiophores.', 900, 'Microbiology'),

('Malaria is caused by which genus of protozoan parasite?', 'Plasmodium', 'Trypanosoma', 'Leishmania', 'Giardia', 'Plasmodium', 'Plasmodium species (P. falciparum, P. vivax, etc.) are obligate intracellular protozoa transmitted by Anopheles mosquitoes.', 750, 'Microbiology'),

-- STERILIZATION & DISINFECTION (easy/medium)
('Which physical method achieves sterilisation by using saturated steam under pressure at 121 degrees C?', 'Autoclaving', 'Pasteurisation', 'Dry heat oven', 'UV irradiation', 'Autoclaving', 'An autoclave uses pressurised steam (121 degrees C, 15 psi, 15-20 min) to denature proteins and kill all microorganisms including spores.', 780, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the difference between sterilisation and disinfection?', 'Sterilisation destroys all microorganisms including spores; disinfection reduces microbial numbers but may not kill spores', 'Disinfection destroys all life; sterilisation reduces only bacterial numbers', 'Sterilisation applies only to liquids; disinfection applies to surfaces', 'They are synonymous terms', 'Sterilisation destroys all microorganisms including spores; disinfection reduces microbial numbers but may not kill spores', 'Sterilisation achieves complete elimination of all viable organisms, whereas disinfection reduces pathogen load to a safe level without necessarily achieving sterility.', 1000, 'Microbiology'),

('Which agent is classified as a high-level disinfectant capable of killing all microorganisms except high numbers of bacterial spores?', 'Glutaraldehyde', 'Isopropyl alcohol (70%)', 'Quaternary ammonium compounds', 'Soap and water', 'Glutaraldehyde', 'Glutaraldehyde is a high-level disinfectant/sterilant that alkylates proteins and nucleic acids, effective against most organisms including mycobacteria.', 1200, 'Microbiology'),

-- ANTIBIOTICS & RESISTANCE (medium/hard)
('Which class of antibiotics inhibits bacterial cell wall synthesis by binding to penicillin-binding proteins (PBPs)?', 'Beta-lactams', 'Aminoglycosides', 'Tetracyclines', 'Macrolides', 'Beta-lactams', 'Beta-lactam antibiotics (penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems) acylate PBPs and block peptidoglycan cross-linking.', 1100, 'Microbiology'),

('How do aminoglycoside antibiotics exert their bactericidal effect?', 'They bind the 30S ribosomal subunit and cause misreading of mRNA', 'They inhibit DNA gyrase', 'They block folic acid synthesis', 'They disrupt the cytoplasmic membrane', 'They bind the 30S ribosomal subunit and cause misreading of mRNA', 'Aminoglycosides irreversibly bind the 30S subunit, inducing codon misreading and producing aberrant proteins that disrupt the membrane.', 1400, 'Microbiology'),

('What is the mechanism of resistance in MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus)?', 'Acquisition of an altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a) with low beta-lactam affinity', 'Production of beta-lactamase that hydrolyses penicillin', 'Efflux pumps that expel methicillin', 'Modification of methicillin by acetyltransferases', 'Acquisition of an altered penicillin-binding protein (PBP2a) with low beta-lactam affinity', 'MRSA carries the mecA gene encoding PBP2a, which has reduced affinity for all beta-lactams and can continue cell wall synthesis in their presence.', 1700, 'Microbiology'),

('Which antibiotic class inhibits bacterial protein synthesis by blocking the 50S ribosomal subunit and preventing translocation?', 'Macrolides', 'Fluoroquinolones', 'Sulfonamides', 'Carbapenems', 'Macrolides', 'Macrolides (erythromycin, azithromycin) bind the 23S rRNA of the 50S subunit, blocking translocation of the growing peptide chain.', 1250, 'Microbiology'),

('Extended-spectrum beta-lactamases (ESBLs) confer resistance by which mechanism?', 'Hydrolysing a broad range of beta-lactam antibiotics including third-generation cephalosporins', 'Modifying the 30S ribosomal target site', 'Pumping antibiotics out of the cell via efflux', 'Methylating the 23S rRNA target', 'Hydrolysing a broad range of beta-lactam antibiotics including third-generation cephalosporins', 'ESBLs are plasmid-encoded enzymes that hydrolyse the beta-lactam ring of penicillins, cephalosporins, and monobactams but are inhibited by clavulanate.', 1800, 'Microbiology'),

-- IMMUNOLOGY BASICS (medium/hard)
('Which cells are the primary mediators of adaptive humoral immunity and produce antigen-specific antibodies?', 'B lymphocytes (plasma cells)', 'Cytotoxic T lymphocytes', 'Natural killer cells', 'Neutrophils', 'B lymphocytes (plasma cells)', 'B cells differentiate into plasma cells upon antigen stimulation, secreting antibodies that neutralise pathogens and opsonise targets.', 1050, 'Microbiology'),

('Which complement pathway is activated directly by bacterial surface molecules without antibody involvement?', 'Alternative pathway', 'Classical pathway', 'Lectin pathway', 'Terminal pathway', 'Alternative pathway', 'The alternative complement pathway is triggered by spontaneous C3 hydrolysis stabilised on microbial surfaces such as LPS, without requiring antibodies.', 1450, 'Microbiology'),

('What is opsonisation in the context of innate immunity?', 'Coating of pathogens with antibodies or complement to enhance phagocytosis', 'Direct killing of bacteria by natural killer cells', 'Release of cytokines to recruit inflammatory cells', 'Formation of the membrane attack complex', 'Coating of pathogens with antibodies or complement to enhance phagocytosis', 'Opsonins (IgG, C3b) coat microbial surfaces and bind Fc or complement receptors on phagocytes, greatly increasing engulfment efficiency.', 1200, 'Microbiology'),

('Toll-like receptors (TLRs) are pattern recognition receptors that detect which type of molecules?', 'Pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs)', 'MHC class II molecules', 'Antigen-antibody complexes', 'Cytokine receptors', 'Pathogen-associated molecular patterns (PAMPs)', 'TLRs on innate immune cells recognise conserved microbial structures (PAMPs) such as LPS, flagellin, and CpG DNA, triggering inflammatory responses.', 1350, 'Microbiology'),

-- NORMAL FLORA (easy/medium)
('Which anatomical site of the human body is normally considered sterile (free of resident microbiota)?', 'Urinary bladder', 'Oral cavity', 'Large intestine', 'Skin', 'Urinary bladder', 'The urinary bladder and its contents are normally sterile; the presence of bacteria indicates urinary tract infection.', 830, 'Microbiology'),

('What term describes the beneficial relationship where normal flora prevents colonisation by pathogens?', 'Colonisation resistance', 'Symbiosis', 'Commensalism', 'Mutualism', 'Colonisation resistance', 'Colonisation resistance describes how indigenous microbiota competitively excludes pathogens through nutrient competition, pH changes, and bacteriocin production.', 1000, 'Microbiology'),

-- PATHOGENESIS & VIRULENCE FACTORS (medium/hard)
('Which toxin produced by Clostridium botulinum causes flaccid paralysis by blocking acetylcholine release at neuromuscular junctions?', 'Botulinum toxin (BoNT)', 'Tetanospasmin', 'Shiga toxin', 'Cholera toxin', 'Botulinum toxin (BoNT)', 'Botulinum toxin cleaves SNARE proteins required for synaptic vesicle fusion, preventing acetylcholine release and causing flaccid paralysis.', 1600, 'Microbiology'),

('What virulence factor allows Streptococcus pyogenes to degrade fibrin clots and spread through tissue?', 'Streptokinase', 'Hyaluronidase', 'Protein A', 'Coagulase', 'Streptokinase', 'Streptokinase (fibrinolysin) activates plasminogen to plasmin, dissolving fibrin clots and enabling bacterial spread through connective tissue.', 1300, 'Microbiology'),

('Endotoxin (LPS) triggers systemic inflammatory response syndrome primarily through stimulation of which cell type?', 'Macrophages', 'B lymphocytes', 'Cytotoxic T cells', 'Mast cells', 'Macrophages', 'LPS binds TLR4 on macrophages, triggering massive cytokine release (TNF-alpha, IL-1, IL-6) that drives systemic inflammation and septic shock.', 1500, 'Microbiology'),

-- BIOFILMS & QUORUM SENSING (hard)
('What is the primary advantage biofilm formation confers on bacteria in clinical settings?', 'Increased resistance to antibiotics and host defences', 'Faster growth rate than planktonic cells', 'Greater nutrient absorption efficiency', 'Enhanced motility for tissue invasion', 'Increased resistance to antibiotics and host defences', 'Biofilm matrix (extracellular polymeric substances) limits antibiotic penetration, reduces oxygen availability, and shields bacteria from phagocytosis.', 1400, 'Microbiology'),

('Quorum sensing coordinates group behaviours in bacteria when autoinducer concentrations exceed which threshold?', 'A population-density-dependent threshold specific to the species', 'A fixed concentration of 10 micromolar', 'The minimum inhibitory concentration of the inducer', 'A threshold set by environmental temperature', 'A population-density-dependent threshold specific to the species', 'Quorum sensing relies on accumulation of species-specific autoinducers; when their concentration reflects sufficient cell density, they bind receptors and activate target genes.', 1700, 'Microbiology'),

-- LAB TECHNIQUES (medium/hard)
('Which culture medium is both selective and differential, used to isolate Gram-negative enteric bacteria while distinguishing lactose fermenters from non-fermenters?', 'MacConkey agar', 'Blood agar', 'Nutrient agar', 'Chocolate agar', 'MacConkey agar', 'MacConkey agar contains bile salts (selective against Gram-positives) and neutral red indicator; lactose fermenters produce pink colonies while non-fermenters remain colourless.', 1150, 'Microbiology'),

('In PCR (polymerase chain reaction), what is the purpose of the denaturation step?', 'To separate the double-stranded DNA template into single strands', 'To allow primers to anneal to the template', 'To extend new DNA from primers using Taq polymerase', 'To introduce dNTPs into the reaction', 'To separate the double-stranded DNA template into single strands', 'Heating to ~94-96 degrees C breaks hydrogen bonds between complementary bases, producing single-stranded DNA templates accessible to primers.', 1100, 'Microbiology'),

('Which technique uses a specific labelled probe hybridised to electrophoretically separated DNA fragments to detect a target gene?', 'Southern blotting', 'Western blotting', 'ELISA', 'Flow cytometry', 'Southern blotting', 'Southern blotting transfers DNA from a gel to a membrane, then uses a complementary labelled probe to detect and locate specific DNA sequences.', 1600, 'Microbiology');




-- Cell membrane transport (easy-medium)
