('Which protein is responsible for pumping 3 Na+ out and 2 K+ into the cell per ATP hydrolysed?', 'Na+/K+-ATPase', 'SERCA pump', 'H+/K+-ATPase', 'Calcium ATPase', 'Na+/K+-ATPase', 'Na+/K+-ATPase (the sodium-potassium pump) maintains the resting electrochemical gradients essential for neuronal excitability.', 820, 'Physiology'),
('Aquaporins are transmembrane channels that facilitate rapid movement of which molecule?', 'Water', 'Glucose', 'Sodium ions', 'Oxygen', 'Water', 'Aquaporins form water-selective pores in cell membranes, enabling rapid osmotic water flux without transporting solutes.', 700, 'Physiology'),

-- Neuron & action potentials (easy-medium-hard)
('What is the typical resting membrane potential of a mammalian neuron?', '-70 mV', '+30 mV', '0 mV', '-55 mV', '-70 mV', 'The resting membrane potential of ~-70 mV is maintained mainly by K+ leakage channels and the Na+/K+-ATPase.', 760, 'Physiology'),
('What determines the conduction velocity of an action potential most strongly in large myelinated axons?', 'Saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier', 'Diameter of the axon alone', 'Density of Na+ channels in the internodal segments', 'Amplitude of the action potential', 'Saltatory conduction between nodes of Ranvier', 'Myelin insulates the axon, forcing the action potential to jump between nodes of Ranvier, greatly increasing conduction speed.', 1200, 'Physiology'),
('During the absolute refractory period, a neuron cannot fire another action potential because:', 'Voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated', 'K+ channels are permanently closed', 'The membrane potential is too positive', 'All neurotransmitters are depleted', 'Voltage-gated Na+ channels are inactivated', 'Na+ channels enter an inactivated (closed but not activatable) state immediately after opening, making re-excitation impossible until they recover.', 1100, 'Physiology'),
('Which of the following correctly describes an EPSP?', 'A graded depolarisation that brings the membrane closer to threshold', 'A hyperpolarisation that inhibits firing', 'An all-or-none potential lasting 100 ms', 'A long-lasting K+ efflux', 'A graded depolarisation that brings the membrane closer to threshold', 'An excitatory postsynaptic potential (EPSP) is a small depolarisation caused by influx of cations, which increases the probability of firing.', 1050, 'Physiology'),

